. **A second falsifier, sharper and cheaper (the fourth seat, 05:4x by the clock, checking an annealed disagreement-percolation criterion whose paths pay a product of actual per-site total variations with frustrated sites rare by their action cost): the free massless Gaussian field.** Couple two such fields with boundary data differing at one far site; their difference is the harmonic extension of the data difference, of order r⁻² in four dimensions and never zero; along the coupling every site's actual total variation is small, there is no frustration, both annealed constants are as good as any theory can make them, and the correlations are algebraic. Van den Berg and Maes's theorem holds because its disagreement probability is the supremum over neighbour configurations, which is of order one for the Gaussian field, and that supremum is what the frustrated datum killed at the crossover; the annealed replacement loses the theorem at the step it names as unknown, since the binary agree/disagree coarse-graining discards the disagreement's amplitude, which in a massless field survives every site. Compact U(1) in its Coulomb phase is the same counterexample with frustration present and rare. Two consequences: any criterion monotone in averaged per-site influences and a frustration density certifies the massless theory first, so none is the mechanism; and partial frustration (one plaquette with tr U_p ≤ 0) costs O(β) and has probability near 0.1 at β = 2.2, so "frustrated links rare" needs its threshold stated and is not small at the crossover for either group. What survives is only the reading in which the site is a block at a scale where the running coupling has entered Theorem 8.7's disk, where the supremum criterion itself closes and nothing annealed is needed: the renormalisation step, the same wall.
