   push(P0(`The quantum Yang–Mills problem of the Clay Mathematics Institute is a single sentence. For every compact simple gauge group *G* it asks for a proof that a non-trivial quantum Yang–Mills theory exists on ℝ⁴ and has a mass gap Δ > 0.[[fn:${S.clay}, §4, p. 6.]] The sentence joins two demands of different kinds. The first is a construction: a system of correlation functions, free of every cutoff, defined on the whole of space-time and obeying axioms at least as strong as those of Wightman and of Osterwalder and Schrader. The second is an inequality: the spectrum of the Hamiltonian of the theory so constructed must leave an interval above zero empty. The inequality cannot be stated before the construction exists, and when the construction proceeds by compactness, the problem's own text, in a footnote examined in Section 3, does not count it as complete until properties of this kind are proved for the limit.`));
