+**The definition, verbatim (p. 264).** "Now we describe the most important expressions in (1.3), (1.6), the β-functions β_(j+1)(g_j). They are determined by the functions E^(j+1)(g_j, U_(j+1)) in (1.6). Let us denote E^(j+1)(g_j, B) = E^(j+1)(g_j, U_(j+1)(exp iB)). We define Π^(ab)_(j+1,μν)(g_j, x, x′) = (δ²/δB^a_μ(x)δB^b_ν(x′)) E^(j+1)(g_j, 0). (1.20) This is the vacuum polarization tensor of the theory defined by the j-th fluctuation field integral. We will analyze this tensor thoroughly in Sect. 5; now we describe its basic properties necessary to formulate a definition of the β-functions. The function E^(j+1) is gauge invariant, hence it is invariant with respect to global transformations V → R(v)V, v ∈ G, or B → R(v)B. … By the Euclidean invariance of E^(j+1) the function (1.20) is Euclidean covariant. This implies Π^(ab)_(j+1,μν)(g_j, x, x′) = δ^(ab) Π_(j+1,μν)(g_j, x − x′), Π_(j+1)(g_j, rb, rb′) = Π_(j+1)(g_j, b, b′), (1.21) … Now we take a limit of these functions as T^(j+1) ↗ ℤ^d. This limit exists by the localized representation (1.7). The function β_(j+1)(g_j) is defined by β_(j+1)(g_j) = −(∂²/∂p₁∂p₂ Π̃_(j+1,12))(g_j, 0) = −(∂²/∂p_μ∂p_ν Π̃_(j+1,μν))(g_j, 0) = Σ_x Π_(j+1,μν)(g_j, x) x_μ x_ν (1.22) for μ, ν arbitrary, μ ≠ ν, where f̃(p) denotes the Fourier transform of the function f(x), x ∈ ℤ^d. It is a smooth function defined on the interval [0, γ], (or analytic), uniformly bounded on this interval together with all derivatives. We will investigate other properties in a separate paper. This completes the description of the inductive assumptions. Now we can formulate a precise version of Theorem 1."
+
+**Theorem 3, verbatim (p. 264).** "There exist positive constants κ₀, M(κ), γ, ε₀, ε₁, α₀, α₁ such, that if κ ≥ κ₀, M ≥ M(κ), 0 < g_k ≤ γ for k = 0, 1, …, K, then the sequence of actions A_k, defined inductively by the small field renormalization transformations (0.17)–(0.20), satisfy all the inductive assumptions described between (1.1)–(1.22). The constants ε₀, ε₁, α₀, α₁ depend on M and satisfy numerous restrictions, which will become clear in the proof. The constant γ depends on all other constants. In the rest of the paper we will be proving this theorem."
